---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/bhali16/Neet150';
---

<Layout
  title="Contributing"
  description="How to report issues and contribute solutions to the NeetCode 150 guide."
>
  <h1>Contributing</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This guide gets better when more people poke holes in it and add to it. Found a mistake in an
    explanation, a bug in a diagram, or want to add a solution in another language? Here's how.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Report an issue</h2>
    <p>
      If something looks wrong — an incorrect trace, a typo, a diagram that doesn't match the
      worked example, or an explanation that's unclear — please
      <a href={`${repoUrl}/issues/new`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open an issue</a
      >. Include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The problem name and section (e.g. "11-graphs / 85-rotting-oranges")</li>
      <li>What's wrong, as specifically as you can</li>
      <li>What you'd expect instead, if you know</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No issue is too small. A one-line typo report is just as welcome as a correctness bug.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Submit a more optimal solution</h2>
    <p>
      Each problem currently ships one worked solution in Python. If you have a cleaner or more
      optimal approach — or a different time/space tradeoff worth showing — open a pull request
      adding it alongside the existing solution rather than replacing it, so readers can compare
      approaches.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Add TypeScript or Java solutions</h2>
    <p>
      Every problem currently only has a Python solution. Solutions in <strong>TypeScript</strong>
      and <strong>Java</strong> are especially welcome — pick any problem file under a section
      directory (e.g. <code>11-graphs/85-rotting-oranges.md</code>) and add a new fenced code
      block under <code>## Optimal Solution</code>, labeled with the language, right after the
      existing Python one:
    </p>
    <pre><code>```typescript
function orangesRotting(grid: number[][]): number {"{"}
  // ...
{"}"}
```</code></pre>
    <p>Keep the same correctness and complexity as the existing solution — just a different language.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Ground rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Verify your fix or addition against the example(s) already in the file before submitting.</li>
      <li>Don't change the problem numbering, section structure, or existing examples.</li>
      <li>Keep explanations beginner-friendly — that's the whole point of this guide.</li>
      <li>One problem/fix per pull request keeps review simple.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="cta">
    <a href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the repository on GitHub &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lede {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  section {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  section h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .cta {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
